  3b. THE PROTOCOL ON THE WIRE (22:11:18, same command, same NIC, 34 s after the v2 capture):
    22:11:18.909061 IP (tos 0xc0, **ttl 64**, id 5603, flags [DF], proto UDP (17), length 112)
        192.168.10.1.520 > **192.168.10.255**.520:
        **RIPv1, Response**, length: 84, routes: 4
          **10.0.0.0**,     metric: 1
          192.168.10.0,     metric: 16
          192.168.48.0,     metric: 1
          192.168.70.0,     metric: 2

    Every documented v1/v2 difference, measured side by side on one wire:

    |                        | version 2 (2e)            | version 1 (3b)                  |
    | ---------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
    | IP destination         | 224.0.0.9 (multicast)     | **192.168.10.255 (broadcast)**  |
    | Ethernet destination   | 01:00:5e:.. (multicast)   | **ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff**           |
    | TTL                    | 1                         | **64**                          |
    | header                 | RIPv2                     | **RIPv1**                       |
    | prefix length / mask   | on every entry            | **absent**                      |
    | route tag, next-hop    | present                   | **absent**                      |
    | AFI field              | AFI IPv4                  | absent                          |
    | entries                | 7                        | **4**                            |
    | 10.0.41.0/30, 10.0.61.0/30, 10.70.70.1/32, 10.90.90.1/32 | four separate entries | **collapsed to one classful `10.0.0.0`, metric 1** |
    | 192.168.70.0/24        | /24 explicit              | 192.168.70.0, mask implied by class C |

    The classful collapse is RIPv1 behaving exactly as it must: advertised out vlan10
    (192.168.10.0/24, a different major network), everything inside 10.0.0.0/8 can only be
    summarised to its classful boundary.  **This is also precisely why the case needed
    192.168.70.0/24**: a class-C prefix whose natural mask IS /24 survives v1 intact, so the v1
    leg measures RIP and not a mask collision (section 5).
    The Ethernet broadcast was caught independently by the counted leg below, whose capture
    holds `UDP 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.255 :520 [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]`
    where every v2 leg's capture held a multicast one.  Two instruments, same answer.

  3c. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE v2 NEIGHBOUR, polled every 30 s:
    22:11:43 .. 22:13:39   R 10.70.70.1/32, R 10.90.90.1/32, R 192.168.70.0/24   (all three)
    **22:14:09            R 10.70.70.1/32, R 192.168.70.0/24 -- 10.90.90.1/32 IS GONE**
    22:14:39, 22:15:09    unchanged (two routes)
    => swi_a's prefix was **timed out ~180 s after the DUT went to version 1**, which is exactly
    the `Timeout after 180 seconds` this platform reports.  Confirmed by the DUT's own view
    (22:15:56):
      awplus# show ip protocols rip
        **Default version control: send version 1, receive version 1**
          vlan10 1 1 · vlan41 1 1 · vlan48 1 1 · vlan61 1 1
        Routing Information Sources:
          Gateway       Distance  Last Update  Bad Packets  Bad Routes
          **10.0.61.2**    120    00:00:27         0            0
                       <- 10.0.41.2 has DROPPED OUT of the sources list entirely
        Number of routes (including connected): 6     (was 7)
    A version-1 receiver does not accept version-2 updates, so the v2-only neighbour ages out.
    **Bad Packets and Bad Routes both stayed 0** -- the v2 updates are not counted as errors,
    they are simply not accepted.

  3d. WHAT SURVIVED v1, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY.  `10.70.70.1/32` -- a /32 -- is
    STILL learned from swi_b under v1, and is still being refreshed (`show ip rip database`
    22:15:57: `R 10.70.70.1/32  10.0.61.2  2  10.0.61.2  vlan61  02:32`, timer resetting).
    That is correct RIPv1: swi_b advertises it on vlan61, whose own address 10.0.61.1/30 is in
    the SAME major network 10.0.0.0/8, so the entry is not summarised at a class boundary and a
    RIPv1 receiver reads an address with a non-zero host part as a HOST route.  The DUT could
    only have derived the /32 that way, because v1 carries no mask -- so the /32 in the database
    IS the evidence of the host-route rule.  **The classful collapse seen in 3b happens on
    vlan10, a different major network; the two observations are the same rule applied to two
    interfaces, not a contradiction.**

